      "title": "Appeasement Fails: Munich Conference",
      "source": "Europe",
      "note": "1938",
      "text": "Adolf Hitler, leader of Germany, promised to expand its boundaries. In 1938, Hitler annexed Austria and then demanded the Sudetenland. At first, France and Great Britain had promised to give Hitler land, but backed out when Hitler threatened war. At the Munich Conference, Britain and France appeased Hitler by giving him part of Czechoslovakia to avoid war. Appeasement is the policy of giving in to someone’s demands (in this case, to avoid war). Hitler became convinced that Britain and France were weak and decided to make more demands.",
